/* POV passage, her side of the task Flirt Duty (task_mom_flirt_duty), stage 2: from her third round on, so it plays
   for every round after that too (a random opening, _fd2). Opened with the CCTV arrow while the task runs, or from her
   end-of-day report. Effects are in the task's onComplete.
   Proposal 5.3: good at it, and a little scary. She knows exactly what she's doing now, and the part of her that likes
   it scares the part that doesn't. All three beats of her report lines happen here: the hardware guy and the glass
   door, the joker going quiet when she sits on his desk, the analyst laughing out loud.
   Clips (images/characters/mom/tasks/, activity flirt_duty2): sitting on the corner of the desk of the joker; the
   analyst laughing; the hardware guy and the glass door */
<<silent>>
	<<SetBackground $locations[13].img>>
	<<set _fd2 to random(0, 2)>>
<</silent>>
<<PovHeader $mom>>
<<if _fd2 == 0>>
I don't need the black monitor by the printer anymore. I know what my lipstick looks like. I know what the skirt does when I walk past the long row of desks, too, because I watched it in the hallway mirror this morning, turning one way and then the other, like a teenager.
<<elseif _fd2 == 1>>
The office is warm again today. It's always a little warm in here, the vent over the main room hums all afternoon, and I've stopped wearing a cardigan to work. I tell myself it's the vent.
<<else>>
I've started looking forward to this part of the day. I noticed it on Tuesday, when the round got moved to the afternoon and I was disappointed all morning, like a girl whose date was pushed back.
<</if>>

I start with $ethan.name. I don't stand next to his desk anymore. I sit on the corner of it, one leg crossed over the other, close enough that he has to choose between looking at his screen and looking at my knee.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "Hey. Hi. So I was going to tell you this thing about the build server, it's actually really funny, there's this..." },
{ text: "I wait. I smile and I wait, and I let my shoe dangle from my toes." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "...there's a server. And it's. Yeah." },
{ who: $mom, text: "It's what, honey?" },
{ who: $ethan, text: "I forgot. I genuinely forgot the joke. That's never happened to me. I've told jokes at funerals." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I pat his hand and get up and leave him to find it again. I don't look back to see if he's watching me go. I don't have to.

$darius.name is harder, he always will be, and that's what makes him fun. He has his whole face arranged before I even get there, polite and a little guarded, a man getting ready to be flattered without letting it count. So I don't flatter him. I tell him his numbers are boring and that I've read all of them, every slide, twice, and that I'm going to need him to explain the one about week six to me again, slowly, over lunch.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $darius, text: "You read the whole deck." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Twice. The footnotes too. Do you know you put a footnote on a footnote? On page nine?" },
{ text: "And he laughs. Out loud, with his head back, the kind of laugh that makes people at the other desks look up. Then he catches himself and looks almost alarmed, as if the laugh had gotten out without asking him." },
{ who: $darius, text: "That was a joke footnote. Nobody has ever found that footnote." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I find out that I like that very much. Being the one who found it.

$brian.name I don't even have to try with anymore. I only walk past his desk on the way to the kitchen, slowly, and say his name once on the way, and he turns his whole chair to follow me and stands up to say something and walks straight into the glass door to the meeting room. Not hard. A soft little bonk, and then a lot of apologizing to the door.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $brian, text: "Sorry. Sorry. That's... it's very clean. The glass. Somebody cleaned it. It's fine. I'm fine." },
{ who: $mom, text: "Let me see, sweetie. Come here." },
{ text: "I put two fingers under his chin and turn his face toward the light to look at his forehead, the way I'd look at anybody's, and he stops breathing completely until I let go." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

Back at my desk I sit very still for a minute with my hands in my lap.

It's so easy. That's the part that frightens me a little. It would be so easy to keep going, to find out how far it goes, and some part of me would like to know, and that part of me is not the part that packs the lunches.

I tell myself I'm doing it for the company. For him. I look across the office, and $player.name looks back, and that's enough to make it true again.

<<EndPov>>
